insert into public.scripture_did_you_know(reference,fact,source_note) values
('Genesis 1:2','The Hebrew word ruach can mean wind, breath, or spirit. Genesis uses it in the opening scene to describe God''s Spirit moving over the waters.','Genesis 1:2; Hebrew רוּחַ (ruach).'),
('Genesis 1:5','In the creation account, a day is described from evening to morning. That repeated rhythm is easy to overlook when reading the chapter quickly.','Genesis 1:5 and the recurring formula in Genesis 1.'),
('Genesis 2:7','In Hebrew, the word for the man, adam, sounds like adamah, the word for ground. The sentence itself links humanity to the earth from which God formed him.','Genesis 2:7; Hebrew אָדָם and אֲדָמָה.'),
('Genesis 3:21','Adam and Eve first made their own coverings from leaves, but later in the same chapter God clothed them with garments of skin. The two coverings are not the same.','Genesis 3:7,21.'),
('Genesis 5:24','Enoch''s story ends without the usual words ''and he died'' found throughout the surrounding genealogy. Genesis instead says God took him.','Genesis 5:21–24; contrast the repeated genealogy formula.'),
('Genesis 7:11','The flood story does not simply say that rain started one day. It gives Noah''s age, the month, and even the day when the waters began.','Genesis 7:11.'),
('Genesis 8:4','Genesis says the ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat, not specifically on the summit of today''s Mount Ararat. The plural wording matters.','Genesis 8:4; Hebrew plural ''mountains of Ararat''.'),
('Genesis 11:4','Ancient Mesopotamian cities built enormous stepped brick towers called ziggurats. Those real buildings help us picture the type of world in Babel''s story, although no excavated tower proves the event itself.','Genesis 11:3–4; archaeological evidence for Mesopotamian ziggurats.'),
('Genesis 12:4','Haran, one of the cities on Abram''s journey, was a real ancient city on important routes in northern Mesopotamia. Its existence is independently known, even though Abram''s visit is recorded in Genesis.','Genesis 11:31–12:4; ancient Harran/Haran.'),
('Genesis 22:2','The name Moriah appears in Abraham''s story. Much later, 2 Chronicles 3:1 uses the same name for the place where Solomon built the Temple.','Genesis 22:2; 2 Chronicles 3:1. A biblical cross-reference.'),
('Genesis 24:22','The jewelry offered during Rebekah''s meeting included a gold nose ring and bracelets. Genesis gives their weights as part of the account.','Genesis 24:22,47.'),
('Genesis 32:28','Jacob is given the name Israel after wrestling. The explanation in the passage connects the name with striving with God and with people.','Genesis 32:28; see also Genesis 35:10.'),
('Genesis 37:3','Joseph''s famous ''coat of many colours'' is one translation of a difficult Hebrew expression. Other Bibles call it a richly ornamented robe or a long robe.','Genesis 37:3; Hebrew ketonet passim; translation comparison.'),
('Exodus 2:3','The Hebrew word tevah is used for both Noah''s ark and the little basket that carried baby Moses. Two rescue stories use the same unusual word.','Exodus 2:3; Genesis 6:14; Hebrew תֵּבָה.'),
('Exodus 14:21','The passage says an east wind blew throughout the night as the sea was driven back. The scene unfolds over time, not merely in a single instant.','Exodus 14:21.'),
('Exodus 15:20','Miriam is explicitly called a prophetess when she leads the women with a tambourine after the crossing of the sea.','Exodus 15:20–21.'),
('Exodus 25:18','The Ark of the Covenant had two cherubim facing one another above its cover. The instructions are surprisingly specific about their wings and placement.','Exodus 25:18–20.'),
('Leviticus 25:10','Jubilee takes its name from the Hebrew yovel, associated with a ram''s horn. The proclamation announced liberty and the return of family property.','Leviticus 25:9–13; Hebrew יוֹבֵל.'),
('Numbers 6:24','The priestly blessing in Numbers is among the biblical texts also found on tiny ancient silver scrolls discovered at Ketef Hinnom near Jerusalem.','Numbers 6:24–26; Ketef Hinnom silver amulets, Israel Antiquities Authority.'),
('Numbers 24:17','Balaam''s oracle joins two royal images: a star rising from Jacob and a sceptre rising from Israel. A star and a ruler appear in the same verse.','Numbers 24:17.'),
('Deuteronomy 6:4','The famous confession beginning ''Hear, O Israel'' is called the Shema because shema is its first Hebrew word. It asks Israel not merely to notice, but to listen to the Lord.','Deuteronomy 6:4; Hebrew שְׁמַע.'),
('Deuteronomy 8:3','When Jesus faced temptation in the wilderness, He quoted this verse about not living by bread alone. One wilderness account speaks directly into another.','Deuteronomy 8:3; Matthew 4:4.'),
('Joshua 10:13','Joshua mentions a written work called the Book of Jashar. The Bible names it, but a complete, authenticated copy of that ancient work has not survived.','Joshua 10:13; 2 Samuel 1:18.'),
('Judges 4:4','Deborah was both a prophetess and a judge in Israel. Scripture describes her hearing disputes under a palm tree, not sitting on a royal throne.','Judges 4:4–5.'),
('Judges 7:2','God explains why Gideon''s army must become smaller: otherwise Israel might claim that its own hand saved it. The number of soldiers is part of the lesson about who receives glory.','Judges 7:2,7.'),
('Ruth 4:7','In Ruth''s time, removing a sandal could publicly confirm a legal transaction. The narrator even pauses to explain the custom to later readers.','Ruth 4:7–8.'),
('1 Samuel 17:40','David picked up five smooth stones from a brook before meeting Goliath. Scripture mentions their number even though it describes only one stone being used.','1 Samuel 17:40,49.'),
('1 Samuel 16:7','A stone inscription found at Tel Dan speaks of the ''House of David''. It is independent ancient evidence for the name of David''s royal dynasty, although it does not retell his anointing.','1 Samuel 16; Tel Dan Stele, Israel Museum.'),
('2 Samuel 7:16','The words ''House of David'' are carved on an ancient Aramaic stone from Tel Dan. A neighboring kingdom remembered the same royal family that Samuel describes.','2 Samuel 7; Tel Dan Stele; Israel Museum.'),
('1 Kings 18:30','Before fire falls, Elijah repairs the altar of the Lord that had been broken down. The repair happens before the dramatic answer.','1 Kings 18:30–39.'),
('2 Kings 9:6','An Assyrian stone called the Black Obelisk names Jehu, a king known from the Bible. You can see the monument today in the British Museum.','2 Kings 9–10; Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III, British Museum.'),
('2 Kings 18:13','The Assyrians carved a huge picture story of their siege of Lachish on palace walls. That city is also named in the biblical account of Sennacherib''s invasion.','2 Kings 18:13–14; Lachish reliefs, British Museum.'),
('2 Chronicles 32:30','A real ancient tunnel carried water into Jerusalem. Its inscription tells how two teams cutting through rock met each other: a striking background to Hezekiah''s waterworks.','2 Chronicles 32:30; Siloam Tunnel inscription.'),
('2 Chronicles 36:23','Cyrus was a real Persian emperor. His clay cylinder describes the return of sacred images to temples after conquering Babylon, providing context for the biblical return from exile, though it does not specifically name Judah.','2 Chronicles 36:22–23; Cyrus Cylinder, British Museum.'),
('Ezra 1:1','The king named in Ezra''s opening sentence, Cyrus of Persia, is also known from ancient inscriptions outside the Bible.','Ezra 1:1; Cyrus Cylinder and Persian royal inscriptions.'),
('Nehemiah 6:15','Nehemiah records that the wall was finished in 52 days. Whatever we make of the chronology, the text presents a specific completion time rather than a vague success story.','Nehemiah 6:15.'),
('Esther 9:26','The Jewish feast of Purim is named after pur, meaning ''lot''. Esther explains the name using the very lots Haman cast in his plot.','Esther 3:7; 9:24–26.'),
('Psalm 119:105','The longest psalm is an acrostic: its sections follow the letters of the Hebrew alphabet. The verse about God''s Word as a lamp belongs to the Nun section.','Psalm 119; Hebrew alphabet headings, especially verses 105–112.'),
('Proverbs 25:21','Paul repeats this proverb in Romans: feed your enemy when he is hungry. The teaching connects wisdom literature to the early church.','Proverbs 25:21–22; Romans 12:20.'),
('Isaiah 7:14','Immanuel is a Hebrew name meaning ''God with us''. Matthew later uses the same name when announcing Jesus'' birth.','Isaiah 7:14; Matthew 1:23; Hebrew עִמָּנוּ אֵל.'),
('Isaiah 44:28','Cyrus appears by name in this prophecy. The Persian king is independently known from ancient historical records and his conquest of Babylon in 539 BC.','Isaiah 44:28–45:1; Cyrus Cylinder.'),
('Jeremiah 36:4','Jeremiah dictated words while Baruch wrote them on a scroll. Archaeologists have found many ancient Judean ink inscriptions showing that professional writing was part of that world.','Jeremiah 36:4; Iron Age Judahite ostraca and seals.'),
('Daniel 5:1','For a long time Belshazzar''s name was known mainly from Daniel. Babylonian records also name Belshazzar as a son of King Nabonidus, though historians distinguish his position from that of his father.','Daniel 5:1; Babylonian Nabonidus records.'),
('Hosea 11:1','Hosea first speaks of Israel as God''s son brought out of Egypt. Matthew later sees that pattern echoed in Jesus'' return from Egypt.','Hosea 11:1; Matthew 2:15.'),
('Joel 2:28','At Pentecost, Peter quotes Joel''s promise that God will pour out His Spirit on sons, daughters, young and old. Joel''s words become the explanation for what the crowd sees.','Joel 2:28–32; Acts 2:16–21.'),
('Jonah 1:2','Nineveh was not an imaginary city. Archaeologists have uncovered its palaces and sculptures. That proves the city''s historical existence, not every episode in Jonah''s journey.','Jonah 1:2; archaeological excavations at Nineveh.'),
('Nahum 3:7','Nineveh really fell in 612 BC, as ancient records and excavations show. Nahum addresses a city whose destruction entered recorded history.','Nahum 3; Babylonian Chronicle and Nineveh excavations.'),
('Habakkuk 2:4','The words ''the righteous shall live by faith'' appear again in Paul''s letters. Romans and Galatians both draw on this one prophetic sentence.','Habakkuk 2:4; Romans 1:17; Galatians 3:11.'),
('Zechariah 9:9','Zechariah pictures a king arriving on a donkey rather than a warhorse. The Gospels intentionally recall that image when Jesus enters Jerusalem.','Zechariah 9:9; Matthew 21:4–5.'),
('Matthew 2:1','Herod the Great was a real ruler. His massive building projects, including the fortress of Masada and work at Caesarea, remain part of the archaeological landscape.','Matthew 2:1; Josephus and Herodian archaeological sites.'),
('Matthew 27:2','A Latin inscription discovered at Caesarea names Pontius Pilate as prefect of Judea. The Roman official in Jesus'' trial is documented outside the Gospels.','Matthew 27:2; Pilate inscription, Israel Museum/Israel Antiquities Authority.'),
('Mark 1:15','The Greek word basileia can describe a king''s reign or royal rule, not only a patch of land. When Jesus announces the Kingdom, He is announcing God''s rule.','Mark 1:15; Greek βασιλεία.'),
('Luke 3:1','Luke names several rulers at the start of John the Baptist''s ministry. Tiberius and Pontius Pilate are independently documented historical people.','Luke 3:1; Roman inscriptions and coins; Pilate stone.'),
('John 5:2','John describes a pool in Jerusalem with five covered colonnades. Excavations have identified a pool complex at Bethesda that helps explain the unusual five-colonnade description.','John 5:2; Bethesda excavations, Jerusalem.'),
('Acts 17:23','Paul began his Athens speech by referring to an altar marked ''To an unknown god''. The idea was intelligible in a city filled with pagan shrines, though that exact altar has not been identified.','Acts 17:23; ancient Athenian religious setting.'),
('Acts 21:28','Ancient Greek warning stones from Jerusalem''s Temple forbade unauthorized people from entering certain sacred courts. They illuminate the accusation made against Paul.','Acts 21:28–29; Jerusalem Temple warning inscriptions.'),
('Romans 8:15','Paul uses the Aramaic word Abba alongside the Greek word for Father. The early church preserved a word from Jesus'' everyday language inside Greek writing.','Romans 8:15; Galatians 4:6; Aramaic אַבָּא.'),
('1 Corinthians 13:4','The Greek word agape names the love Paul describes. Notice that he does not stop at a definition: he shows how love behaves.','1 Corinthians 13:4–7; Greek ἀγάπη.'),
('Galatians 5:22','Paul writes about the ''fruit'' of the Spirit in the singular, then names several qualities. The grammar presents them as one harvest, not nine unrelated prizes.','Galatians 5:22–23; Greek καρπός (karpos), singular.'),
('Ephesians 2:14','The Temple in Jerusalem had a real dividing barrier, and surviving Greek warning inscriptions show how seriously access was restricted. Paul''s ''dividing wall'' image had a visible setting.','Ephesians 2:14; Temple warning inscription, archaeological collections.'),
('Philippians 4:22','Paul sends greetings from believers connected to ''Caesar''s household''. That does not mean Caesar became a Christian; it shows how far the Christian community had spread.','Philippians 4:22; Roman imperial household context.'),
('Colossians 4:16','Paul asks for a letter from Laodicea to be read at Colossae. The verse reminds us that the New Testament mentions correspondence beyond the letters that survive today.','Colossians 4:16.'),
('2 Timothy 4:13','Paul asks Timothy to bring his cloak, books, and especially the parchments. This small request brings ordinary travel, learning, and writing into the prison story.','2 Timothy 4:13.'),
('Revelation 21:21','Revelation describes twelve gates as pearls. The picture is part of John''s vision, not a claim that archaeologists have found the city''s walls.','Revelation 21:21; visionary symbolism.'),
('Revelation 22:2','The Bible begins with a tree of life in Eden and ends with the tree of life appearing again. The final vision deliberately echoes the opening garden.','Genesis 2:9; Revelation 22:2.')
